{% extends "_layout.html.twig" %}
{% import "_makros.html.twig" as makros %}
{% block title %}Contributing icon changes{% endblock %}
{% block content %}

    {%- include '_breadcrumb.html.twig' -%}

    <h1>Contributing icon changes</h1>
    <p class="sds-lead">
        A successful icon change solves a specific interface problem while still looking like part of
        the TYPO3 set. Work from the source SVG, use the established construction rules and validate
        the generated asset in context before opening a pull request.
    </p>

    {%- include '_toc.html.twig' -%}

    {#- The rhythm between the sections, stated by the container Soul has
        for it. Left to the flow, the air above a heading is a rule on the
        block before it, and a block whose own margin ties with that rule
        wins on source order — a table before a heading closed the two to
        the step a paragraph gets. -#}
    <div class="sds-section">
        <h2>Visual language</h2>
        <p>
            Search the library before drawing. An existing identifier may already express the intended
            action, object or status, and reusing it keeps interfaces predictable. If an existing drawing
            needs refinement, preserve its identifier unless the meaning itself changes; identifiers are
            an API used by TYPO3 Core and extensions.
        </p>
        <p>
            Compare nearby icons in the same category, then read the relevant guidance for
            <a href="{{ pathPrefix }}guide/drawing.html">action and overlay icons</a> or
            <a href="{{ pathPrefix }}guide/modules.html">module icons</a>. The canvas, protective space,
            stroke weight and use of <code>currentColor</code> should be deliberate rather than corrected
            after export.
        </p>
    </div>
    <div class="sds-section">
        <h2>Edit the source, not the output</h2>
        <p>
            Source SVGs live under <code>src/&lt;category&gt;/&lt;identifier&gt;.svg</code>. The folder places the
            icon in its category and the filename becomes its public identifier. Files under
            <code>dist/</code> are generated by the icon build and must not be treated as the authoring
            source.
        </p>
        <p>
            Keep SVG markup small and portable. Use the correct <code>viewBox</code>, avoid embedded raster
            images and editor-specific metadata, and use <code>currentColor</code> where the icon should
            inherit its context. Module accent elements follow the dedicated module-icon guidance.
        </p>
    </div>
    <div class="sds-section">
        <h2>Metadata and compatibility</h2>
        <p>
            Matching YAML files under <code>meta/&lt;category&gt;/</code> hold aliases, tags and version history.
            When an identifier replaces an older name, an alias can preserve discovery and compatibility; do
            not remove an identifier casually once it has shipped.
        </p>
        <p>
            Version history is generated from Git tags. Review it as release data, not as a substitute for
            the commit history. Compatibility across TYPO3 branches depends on the package version in which
            the identifier first appears.
        </p>
    </div>
    <div class="sds-section">
        <h2>Tags</h2>
        <p>
            Tags are what the search finds an icon by, next to its identifier and its aliases. Every word of
            a query has to appear in one of them, so a tag is only useful if it is a word somebody would
            type when looking for the icon. Give every icon three to eight tags, drawn from three kinds:
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>What the icon depicts: <em>trash can</em>, <em>clock</em>, <em>flag</em>, <em>chain</em>.</li>
            <li>What it stands for in TYPO3, in TYPO3's own terms: <em>deleted records</em>,
                <em>scheduler task</em>, <em>page tree</em>, <em>backend layout</em>.</li>
            <li>Synonyms people type for the same thing: <em>delete</em> and <em>remove</em>,
                <em>settings</em> and <em>configuration</em>.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            Write tags in English and in lowercase. A phrase such as <em>broken link</em> is a single tag.
            Leave out words the identifier already contains; it is searched anyway. Use the same words as
            the icons around it for the same concept, so a search for <em>delete</em> finds every icon
            that deletes. <code>npm run verify</code> checks the count, the case and the words of the
            identifier, and CI runs it on every push.
        </p>
        {{ makros.codeblock('alias: []
    tags:
      - trash
      - bin
      - deleted records
      - restore
      - undelete', 'yaml') }}
    </div>
    <div class="sds-section">
        <h2>Build and inspect the result</h2>
        <p>Install dependencies once, then rebuild the distributed icon assets:</p>
        {{ makros.codeblock('npm ci
    npm run icons-build', 'bash') }}
        <p>
            Build the site when the generated preview matters to the review. Inspect the normal, disabled
            and inverse contexts supported by the category, and judge the icon at its real rendered size—not
            only enlarged in an SVG editor.
        </p>
        {{ makros.codeblock('npm run site-build
    npm run site-serve', 'bash') }}
    </div>
    <div class="sds-section">
        <h2>Pull-request checklist</h2>
        <ul>
            <li>The identifier and category describe the icon's meaning.</li>
            <li>The metadata file carries three to eight tags that follow the tag rules.</li>
            <li>The SVG follows the correct grid and remains legible at its intended size.</li>
            <li>Colors and directionality behave correctly in supported contexts.</li>
            <li>Generated assets were rebuilt and inspected.</li>
            <li>The pull request explains the interface need and includes useful previews.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

{% endblock %}
